E[1] = """Document no. 20

Mieszko, Duke of Poland, 31 August 1173, at Gniezno; gives the estate of Wrąbczynek to the monastery of Ląd.

Original on parchment. The lower edge, folded over, shows 4 small holes for hanging the seal, in one of them threads of yellow silk. The script seems to be of the first half of the thirteenth century. The seal is lost.

Library of the Princes Czartoryski. Diplomata, vol. I, no. 1.

In the name of the Lord, amen. Forgetting is a stepmother to memory, and the course of deeds is darkened by the length of time, unless it is made known to those who come after by the living tokens of letters. Therefore I, Mieszko, by the grace of God Duke of Poland, make known to those present and to come that, meaning to increase the favour and grace which I bear especially towards the brothers of the Cistercian Order of the house of Ląd, of our foundation, so that they may the better persevere in the service of the name of God, at the request of the pious father Lord Bertold, abbot of that monastery, and of his devout convent, I have given them our estate called little Vronchin, with salt, lakes, fishing and hunting, and the other uses there at present and to come, free of all the rights of Poland by whatever names they are called, to be possessed by hereditary right. Witnesses of this are: Jarost, palatine of Kalisz; Bronis, castellan of Gniezno; Odolan the cupbearer; Ilik the chaplain; and many others worthy of trust. Given at Gniezno in the year of the Lord one thousand one hundred and seventy-three, on the day before the Kalends of September, in the sixth indiction. And that this may remain valid and firm, we have had the present document strengthened by the hanging of our seal.

Lendensis, that is of Ląd. Parvum Vronchin, today Wrąbczynek."""

E[2] = """Document no. 222

Fulko, Archbishop of the church of Gniezno, 1240 (sic), 30 September, without place; gives the monastery of Ląd the tithes of the chapels of St Peter and St Andrew.

Poznań. Royal State Archive. Ląd, Liber privilegiorum B, no. 38.

In the name of the Lord, amen. Since we must stand before the judgement seat of Christ on the last day, to receive good or evil according to what we have done in the body, it is fitting that we prepare ourselves a path to the homeland of eternal life by works of mercy. Therefore we, Fulko, by the grace of God Archbishop of the holy church of Gniezno, wish it to be known to all who will look on this page that, when the illustrious prince Lord Władysław, son of Odo, Duke of Poland, was settling the pressing business of the land with his barons at the monastery of Ląd on the day before St Remigius, it happened that, moved by God, he considered with the eye of piety the many burdens of the abbot and of the brothers serving God there, and, giving them the site of the castle that was called Lenda with its appurtenances, he assigned with most earnest devotion to their authority the chapels of St Andrew and St Peter, which had been of his patronage. And since it happened that we, called by the same prince, were present at this assembly together with our venerable brother and fellow bishop Lord Paweł of Poznań, moved by the prayers of the said prince and the most devoted entreaties of the barons, and pierced to the heart by the wretched poverty of the abbot and his brothers, we have given the tithes of the said chapels to God and to the blessed Mary ever Virgin, for the remedy of our soul and of our successors, for the use of the brothers, to be possessed for ever as they now are and as they may be in future, excepting no profit that may arise from their enlargement. And so that our devout gift may not be undone by any of our successors, we have named the estates in which the said tithes, with all their profits, are to fall to them: from Zagórów, Skokum, Kopojno, Trąbczyn, Szetlew, Łukom and Wrąbczyn; which tithes, by whatever right they are raised, whether the villages are divided or moved or called by another name, we give gladly, whatever of our right there may be, for the use of our brothers. And that all this may remain ratified and unshaken for ever, we strengthen the present writing by hanging our seal and that of the Chapter and that of our most beloved brother Lord Paweł, the bishop named above, who was present, and confirm it also by the witness of faithful men, whose names are these: Gotpold, the greater provost; Hugo, the dean; Mikołaj, the custodian; Wacław, the scholastic; and very many others worthy of trust. Given and done in the year of the Lord 1240 (sic), on the second day before the Kalends of October, in the twelfth indiction.

Landensis, of Ląd. Zagurow, Skokom, the same today. Kopanino, later Kopasino, today Kopojno. Trąbczyn, Szetlewo, Łukomie, the same today. Wronczino, today Wrąbczyn."""

E[3] = """Document no. 238

Przemysł, Duke of Poland, 4 May 1243, in the church of the hospital at Gniezno; together with his brother Duke Bolesław hands over the hospital at Gniezno, most amply endowed with goods, to the rule of the Holy Sepulchre of the house of Miechów.

Poznań. Royal State Archive. Fasciculus Mączniki, B. IV. (A copy made in the 18th century from the original, which bore a seal.) Nakielski, Miechovia.

In the name of the holy and undivided Trinity, amen. It is plain to all mortals how they are tossed by the turning of the times: that a long stretch of time gives birth to age, age to forgetting, and forgetting to the undoing of things done; for which reason it is needful that a thing worthy of memory be committed to writing, so that the greying past may be the more often renewed. I therefore, Przemysł, by the grace of God Duke of Poland, with my younger brother Bolesław and with our beloved mother Lady Jadwiga, make known to those present and to come who look on the present writing that, to the honour of God, in memory and for the remedy of our souls, with the consent of our venerable father Lord Fulko, Archbishop of Gniezno, and of his Chapter, and on the advice of our barons, we have given our hospital of Gniezno to the Holy Sepulchre, to be possessed by perpetual right under the rule and government of Lord Henryk, provost of the house of Miechów, and of his successors: for which the said provost shall be bound to provide through an honest and prudent man, a diligent rector, so that the alms given to the said hospital are not neglected or spent to no purpose. But if there should be, which God forbid, such a master as proved useful neither to God nor to his house, he shall be removed at our request and that of the Lord Archbishop and of his Chapter, if he has been canonically convicted and proved so, and another fit master shall be put in his place by the provost of the house named above, who will know how to keep favour and honour as far as God allows. In the hospital named above, as we have said, for the remedy of the souls of our forebears and successors and of our own, once it has been restored to a good state, all the sick in need of alms, not robbers, not men of violence, are to be cared for as far as the house can. Some poor scholars too shall be kept there at its cost, in food, not in clothing, at the will of the master of the hospital. And because, as our sins deserve, in these modern times when the little fire of charity grows cold on earth, there are found more invaders of spiritual houses than helpers or defenders, we wish to be true heirs and defenders in what belongs to the lay hand, and the Lord Archbishop with his Chapter in spiritual things. Done in the year of grace 1243 in the church of the said hospital at Gniezno on the feast of St Florian, in the presence of these witnesses: Lord Jan the dean, Krystian the custodian, Andrzej, Tomasz, Stefan the duke's chancellor, Wincenty and his brother Piotr, Izajasz and Piotr, canons of Gniezno, and many other canons and vicars; and these barons: Bogumił and Dzierżykraj, palatines of Poland; Cieciert, castellan of Gniezno; Tomasz, castellan of Poznań; Domarat the judge, and many other knights; the Dominican friars Jerzy and Piotr, and Artung, by whose mediation this is known to have been done. These are the villages of this hospital: Zdziechowa with those assigned to it, Lachowo, Imielno, Pawłowo with the lake called Prenevo, and Przysieka, Cielimowo, Ujazd, inns at Kłecko, and Ielemno with the whole lake and a mill and with beavers to be taken freely, Stasin too with the lake called Cubuchowo and with all its uses, Lozine, Konikowo, and a mill and an orchard and a field by the orchard and by the hospital. These are the villages excepted into one neighbourhood, which are free: Koreczno, Przysieka, Jezierzany, Zdziechowa, Ujazd; so that the huntsmen of any office are not to be received there, nor are they to feed our dogs or give carts, and we make them wholly free of every military expedition and the building of castles and of all other exactions and payments.

Hospitale Scti Sepulcri, the so-called Bożogrobcy (canons of the Holy Sepulchre). Mechoviensis, of Miechów. Zdechova, Zdziechowa. Leccovo, Lachowo. Chemelino, Imielno. Pawlowo, Pawłowo. Preseca, Przysieka. Cymellowo, Cielimowo. Ugasd, Ujazd. Clech, Kłeck. Ielemno cum pleno lacu et molendino, once probably a settlement on the river Wełna near the town of Kłecko, today vanished. Stasin, Lozine, not identified. Conicowo, Konikowo. Kurrino, Koreczno. Iezesani, Jezierzany."""

E[4] = """Document no. 393

Bolesław, Duke of Poland, 10 December 1261, at Sechowa; confirms the grants of estates and liberties made to the monastery of Ląd by Mieszko, Duke of Poland, in 1145 on the occasion of its foundation, and adds new ones on his own part.

Poznań. Royal State Archive. Ląd, Liber privilegiorum B, no. 4. (Compare charter no. 10, and Rzyszczewski and Muczkowski, Codex diplomaticus Poloniae I, no. 1.)

In the name of the holy and undivided Trinity, amen. Since every deed of men of our time is wont to be held doubtful that is not strengthened by the points of letters, we Bolesław, by the grace of God Duke of Poland, ordered the brothers of the house of Ląd of the Cistercian Order to show us their privileges on the foundation of that house. Having examined them with due care, we found in them with full certainty that our great-grandfather Mieszko, by the grace of God great Duke of Poland, having founded the said house in the year of the Lord 1145, for the support of the brothers serving God there, to the honour of our Lord Jesus Christ and of his glorious mother Mary ever Virgin, bestowed on the said house these estates with their people and all their uses present and future: namely Kościół, where he granted that there should be a market full of every liberty, Dolany, Mokra, Kłobia, Choceń. The people of these villages are bound each to pay on the feast of St Michael sixty sheaves of rye, a measure of oats, a measure of wheat, three urns of malt; to reap five heaps of corn, three cartloads of hay and as many of wood, two pine trunks; for three days on end with all the household to clean the crops, each to thresh for one day, and to give three bundles of flax. He also gave these estates listed below with their people and all their uses present and future: Szetlew with the cooks, Rzgów with the sanctuary people and the stable men, Grabienice with the chamberlains, Sławsk with the fishermen, Wiramowo, Kwaskowo, Chorzeń, Staralanka, Swiniarowo. The people of these villages are bound to draw the net between Sieradz and Władysław, and throughout all Poland, whenever the need of the brothers requires, to carry their orders, on horse as well as on foot. He granted too that the fishermen and huntsmen of the said brothers might freely follow fishing and hunting wherever his fishermen and huntsmen and those of his successors go. He also gave the said brothers, for providing lights, three markets with inns, namely Glev, Thur and Sobotka. He would also have the innkeepers of the said villages summoned nowhere but before the abbot. And since a gift given to God ought not to be subject to the laws of princes, he freed all the people living in the said villages from carting, from every conveyance, from watch, from tribute, naraz, podworowe, market and bridge tolls, from the building of castles or of any bridges, from receiving, guarding or escorting beaver-keepers, from the guarding and payment of falcons, which is called sokol, from the neighbourhood called in the common tongue opole, from every exaction or levy, from carting and military expeditions, and from all the rights established in Poland by whatever names they are called. Reserving to no judge or castellan any right to summon or judge the said people or their villages. But since the age of time does not let things which cannot be preserved by keeping stay in the same state, and it was to be feared that, the said privilege of our great-grandfather being worn away by great age, harm might in future arise to the said house, we, following with pious affection the devotion of our forebears, confirm the said estates with the people and liberties written above, and take moreover into our protection the estates given to them by hereditary right by the nobles of Poland, namely Skarboszewo, Marcinkowo, Jaroszyn, Górka, Zagórów, Skokum, Oleśnica. And for the remission of our sins we free all the people living in the said villages, bondsmen, free men and hired men serving in their courts, from every Polish right and from the levy which is called powolne (sic). We have also given the said brothers the water of the Warta with the streams adjoining it, from the bounds of the old castle as far as Drążyn of the bishop and on the other side as far as Kopojno, with the fishing, uses and beavers, to be possessed by hereditary right. And so that this our deed, to the honour of God and of his holy mother ever Virgin Mary, may be kept inviolably ratified, we have had the present document strengthened by the protection of our seal, on this condition: that if, which God forbid, any of our successors or of others should dare with rash daring to go against this our deed, he shall know that he will incur the indignation of almighty God and of the blessed apostles Peter and Paul and of all the Saints. Done at Sechowa in the year of the Lord 1261, on the fourth day before the Ides of December. There is also the witness of faithful men: Jan, by the grace of God Archbishop of Gniezno; Jan the chancellor; Erkenbold, palatine of Gniezno; Szymon, castellan of Gniezno; Mateusz, castellan of Ląd; Mateusz our notary, who also sealed the present document.

Landensis, of Ląd. Koscol, Kościół of St Nicholas, the place on which later stood the town of Lądek. Dolan, Dolany. Moscho, in no. 10 Morsk, Mokra, a vanished settlement near the town of Kazimierz, or else Mokre near the town of Rychwał. Cloba, Kłobia. Gocszene, as later appears, Choceń. Szethowo, Szetlewo. Coci, that is the village of the cooks, Kuchary Kościelne. Rzgowo, Rzgów. Sanctuarii, that is the village of the sanctuary people, Świątniki. Stabularii, perhaps the vanished settlement of Obory. Grabienice, Grabienice. Camerarii, perhaps the vanished settlement of Komorniki. Slavsko, Sławsk. Piscatores, perhaps a separate, vanished settlement, Rybitwy. Wiramowo, Kwaskowo, not identified. Chozen, Chorzeń. Staralanka, Swiniarowo, not identified. Seradz, Sieradz. Wladislav, Władysławów. Glev, probably Galewo. Thur, Tury. Sobotka, Sobotka. Skarbossewo, Skarboszewo. Marcinkowo, later renamed Kowalewo. Iarossyno, Jaroszyn. Gorzyza, Gorka. Zagorowo, Zagurów. Skokom, Skokum. Oleszyno, probably Oleśnica. Antiquum castrum, stood beside the monastery of Ląd. Dretino episcopi, Drążyn. Capayn, Kopojno. Sechowa, Czechowo, or else Szetlewo."""

E[5] = """Document no. 460

Bolesław, Duke of Poland, 27 March 1276, at Dłusk; gives Count Mikołaj, treasurer of Ląd, the village of Trąbczyn.

Original on parchment. On thick cords of faded silk thread three seals are hung. The first is Duke Bolesław's (seal XVI); the second is lost, only the strand of silk thread remains; the third is Duke Przemysł's (seal XXI).

Library of the Princes Czartoryski. Diplomata.

In the name of the Lord, amen. While the letter lives, the deed committed to it lives, whose assertion nourishes memory and, strengthened, takes increase. Therefore we Bolesław, by the grace of God Duke of Poland, together with our beloved little son Duke Przemysł and with our beloved wife Lady Jolenta, having regard to the faithful services of Count Mikołaj, our treasurer of Ląd, have finally and with pure intent given him our village which is called Trupcino, to be possessed by him and his descendants by hereditary right for ever .. (sic) although we had once given the said village to Count Janko son of Szymon, who died having no offspring, and so the said village came back to us .. (sic) and we had given the named village to Lord Mikołaj, who has gone the way of all flesh and had no lawful successors .. and so of right we have given the village named before to the said Count and his successors. And we free the said village altogether from these payments: from the ox, from the cow, from the opole, from the castle, from the poradlne, from the watch, from the podworowe, from carting, from conveyance, from the naraz. The privilege of Janko of blessed memory shall be of no force, as also that of the said Mikołaj, formerly parish priest of Środa .. And so that this our gift, which has come forth so duly from us all, may not be undone by anyone in the course of time, we have had the present writing strengthened by the protection of our seals, in the presence of these witnesses: Count Jarkelbold, palatine of Gniezno; Count Sykstus, castellan of Ruda .. Count Żegota, huntsman of Kalisz; Count Berwold, cupbearer of Ląd .. Count Sędziwój, under-chamberlain of Gniezno; Świętosław our scribe. Given in the year of the Lord 1276, written at Dłusk on the sixth day before the Kalends of April, on the eve of St Priscus the martyr and of Malchus.

Lenda, Ląd. Trupcino, Trąbczyn. Sroda, Środa. Rudensis, of Ruda. Dluscho, Dłusk."""

E[6] = """Document no. 27

Kalisz, 18 October 1280

Duke Przemysł II confirms the possessions of the Cistercian monastery at Ląd and endows them with certain liberties.

Original: Cologne, Historisches Archiv, Kl. Lond I, 15. Parchment 485 × 350+17 mm. On a pink silk cord the issuer's seal, showing (according to the editor in DKM) the standing figure of the duke holding a spear in his right hand and in his left a shield with a rampant lion; on both sides of the duke stand armed men blowing horns [probably on towers]. The description recalls the seal of Przemysł I, and of II (compare KDW IV, seal no. XV). On the back: Renovacio privilegii ducis Mesiconis (15th century).

Copy: The same, Copiarius, fols 15–16 (15th century).

Edition: DKM, pp. 356–358, no. 8 (from the original).

Regests: 1. Perlbach, Lond, p. 86 (from the original); 2. Krzyżanowski, Dyplomy, p. 168 (from edition 1).

|| In the name of the Lord, amen. || Since we are to pass with the swiftness of our days, as a shadow is wont to flee or smoke to vanish, | therefore we, Przemysł the Second, by the grace of God Duke of Poland, wish it to be known to all the faithful of Christ, present as well as to come, that for the love of our Lord Jesus Christ, the liberator of our | souls, and for the veneration of his glorious mother, Mary perpetual Virgin, and of All Saints, we renew for the brothers beloved in Christ who serve the Lord in the house of Ląd the privilege of our glorious | great-grandfather, the lord Mieszko, Duke of Poland¹, confirming to the said brothers these estates written below with their people and all the uses that are in them and may come in future, taking them into our protection and committing them to be defended by our successors for all time, in sole regard of divine reward. Now the places of the possessions are written below: namely Kościół², where we confirm that there shall be a market full of every liberty, Dolany³, Morsko⁴, Kłobia⁵ and Choceń⁶. The people of these villages are bound each to pay on the feast of St Michael [29 IX] sixty sheaves of rye, a measure of oats, a measure of wheat, three urns of malt, to reap five heaps of corn, three cartloads of hay and as many of wood, two pine trunks, for three days on end with all the household to clean the crops, each to thresh for one day, and to give three bundles of flax. We confirm too these estates written below with their people and all the uses that are in them and may be hereafter: Szechouo with the cooks⁷, Irgouo with the sanctuary people and the stable men⁸, Grabonowiz with the chamberlains⁹, Slausco with the fishermen¹⁰, Wiranouo¹¹, Quascouo, ¹², Choren¹³, Staralancha¹⁴, Suinareuo¹⁵. The people of the said villages are bound to draw the net within Sieradz¹⁶ and Włocławek¹⁷, and throughout all Poland¹⁸, whenever the need of the brothers requires, to carry their orders, on horse as well as on foot. We confirm moreover that the fishermen and huntsmen of the said brothers may freely follow fishing and hunting wherever our fishermen and huntsmen and those of our successors go. We confirm too to the said house, for providing lights, three markets with inns: Chlew¹⁹, Thur²⁰, Sobotka²¹; we confirm too that the innkeepers of these villages are summoned nowhere but before the abbot. And since a gift given to God ought not to be subject to the laws of princes, we free all the people living in the said villages from carting, from every conveyance, from watch, from tribute, from the naraz, podworowe, market and bridge tolls, from the building of castles or of any bridges, from receiving, guarding or escorting beaver-keepers, from receiving huntsmen of any kind, from the payment and guarding of falcons, which is called sokol, from the neighbourhood called in the common tongue opole, from every exaction or levy, from carting and military expeditions, and from all the rights established in Poland by whatever names they are called. Reserving to no castellan or judge any right to summon or judge the said people or their villages; but when a grave cause arises which cannot fittingly be settled before the abbot or his judge, they are to be summoned to our presence by our ring, the abbot to have whatever falls by right of judgement; and they are to be imprisoned nowhere but in the abbot's stable. And so that this our confirmation, to the honour of God and of his holy mother ever Virgin Mary, may have the strength of lasting firmness, we have had the present document strengthened by the protection of our seal. Done at Kalisz on the feast of St Luke the Evangelist in the year of the Lord @1280@, in the presence of these witnesses, namely: Lord Andrzej, archdeacon and chancellor of Kalisz²², Count Żegota, huntsman of Kalisz²³, Count Piotrek, cupbearer of Poznań²⁴, Count Naszan, equerry of Kalisz²⁵, Count Falisz, procurator of Kalisz²⁶, and given by the hand of Tylo, notary of our court²⁷.

¹Mieszko III (d. 1202) was the great-great-grandfather of Przemysł II. The document mentioned here is a forgery dated 23 April 1145 (KDW I, no. 10), kept in the form of a supposed original, in truth made in the 13th century, in the archive of Ląd, today in Cologne, Historisches Archiv, Kl. Lond I/1; compare also on it Z. Kozłowska-Budkowa, Repertorium, pp. 43–45. The present document repeats a large part of the document of supposedly 1145.

²Kościół, commonly identified with Lądek, east of Ląd.

³Dolany, see above, no. 26/4.

⁴Morsko, see above, no. 13/4.

⁵Kłobia, a parish village in Kujawy, south of Brześć.

⁶Choceń, a parish village in Kujawy, north of Chodecz, south-east of Kłobia.

⁷Probably Szetlew, south-east of Zagórów; but compare Kuchary Kościelne, 9 km east of Szetlew.

⁸Rzgów, north-east of Szetlew. Świątniki just west of Rzgów.

⁹Grabienice, just south of Rzgów.

¹⁰Sławsk on the Warta, west of Konin.

¹¹Wiranowo and Kwaskowo not identified; they occur also in the forgery of 1145.

¹²Wiranowo and Kwaskowo not identified; they occur also in the forgery of 1145.

¹³Chorzeń on the Warta, north-west of Konin, today within the town of Konin.

¹⁴Stara Łąka not identified; it occurs also in the forgery of 1145.

¹⁵Perhaps Świniarzewo (Siniarzewo), a parish village in Kujawy, north-west of Brześć, a village named also in the forgery of 1145.

¹⁶Sieradz.

¹⁷Włocławek.

¹⁸Polonia probably in the sense of Greater Poland.

¹⁹Chlew (rather this, not Glew), Tur and Sobótka are hard to identify; compare Zajączkowscy, Materiały II, pp. 108–109.

²⁰Chlew (rather this, not Glew), Tur and Sobótka are hard to identify; compare Zajączkowscy, Materiały II, pp. 108–109.

²¹Chlew (rather this, not Glew), Tur and Sobótka are hard to identify; compare Zajączkowscy, Materiały II, pp. 108–109.

²²Andrzej Zaremba, see above, no. 26/8.

²³Żegota, see above, no. 13/7.

²⁴Piotrek, Pietrzyk, son of Prandota, steward of Poznań 1275, cupbearer 1280, castellan of Poznań 1284, palatine of Poznań 1297–1299 (Spisy wlkp.).

²⁵Naszan, equerry of Kalisz 1280–1288 (Spisy wlkp.).

²⁶Chwalisz (Falisz), see above, no. 26/11.

²⁷Tylo, see above, no. 26/12."""

E[7] = """Document no. 678

Przemysł the Second, Duke of Greater Poland, 21 October 1291, at Gniezno; confirms the exchange of the estates of the monastery of Ląd called Głowiew, Sławsk and Chocicza for the estate of Count Sędziwój the under-chamberlain called Kopojno, which he endows with the most ample privileges.

Original on parchment. On a thick cord of green silk thread hangs the seal of Duke Przemysł (seal XXVI).

Library of the Princes Czartoryski. Diplomata. Rzyszczewski and Muczkowski, Codex diplomaticus Poloniae I, no. 77; from another original, which has at the end: Data per Iasconem notarium curie nostre anno Domini M.CC. nonagesimo primo in Gnezna, in die XI millium Virginum.

In the name of the Lord, amen. Since the forgetfulness inborn in human memory leads into error the settlements of temporal acts that have not been firmly strengthened by the point of letters or the subscription of witnesses, we, Przemysł the Second, by the grace of God Duke of Greater Poland, wish it to be known to all the faithful of Christ, present as well as to come, that the venerable abbot Lord Gerhard of Ląd and his convent made an exchange with our beloved under-chamberlain Count Sędziwój for the good of their house, with our gracious favour and consent, giving him three estates, namely Głowiew, Sławsk and Chocicza, for the estate called Kopojno, which the same Sędziwój had held by inheritance, exchanged with us for his patrimony, namely Górczyn, free and exempt from every Polish right by whatever name it is called; the sons of the said Sędziwój, namely Janko and Marcin, to whom the hereditary right belonged, resigning the said estate before us and yielding up the hereditary right, and the said abbot with his brothers entering the said estate of Kopojno to possess it by inheritance. And we, desiring to increase the affection which we have always had for the house of Ląd, will that the said estate with its inhabitants, their own or free, shall be exempt and free, as it was before when it was held by the said Sędziwój: from every conveyance, from carting, from watch, from tribute, from the naraz, from the podworowe, from market and bridge tolls, from the building of castles or of any bridges, from receiving, guarding or escorting beaver-keepers (sic), for we have given the beavers to the lord abbot and his brothers to hunt for ever. Moreover from the neighbourhood which is called in the common tongue opole, from receiving the under-huntsman or huntsmen of any kind, from the guarding or payment of falcons, which is popularly called socol, from every exaction or levy which is called povolove, from carting and military expeditions, and from all the rights established in Poland by whatever names they are called. We have given the said brothers, through their own judge, free authority to carry out all judgements, such as of strife, striking, maiming of limbs, or even beheading, so that we may have the greater trust towards God at the dreadful judgement, reserving to no castellan or judge any right to summon or judge in that estate. And that this our deed, to the honour of God and of holy Mary ever Virgin, may obtain the strength of lasting firmness, we strengthen the present page with the protection of our seal. Witnesses of this are: Filip, archdeacon of Gniezno; Berwold, castellan of Kalisz; Abraham, treasurer of Gniezno. These things were done in the year of the Lord 1291 at Gniezno, on the day of the Eleven Thousand Virgins.

Lenda, Ląd. Glovevo, Głowiew. Slavsco, Sławsk. Chociczha, Chocicza. Copaino, Kopojno. Gorcino, Górczyn."""
